  <section class="ll-sec" id="objections">
    <div class="ll-wn">
      <div class="ll-sec-hd center">
        <span class="ll-kicker">Transparent Answers</span>
        <h2>What People Are Already Asking Online</h2>
        <p class="ll-lead">These are the real questions from thousands of people who saw LitterLoops™. We answer each one honestly — including the limitations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ll-obj-list">

        <!-- open by default -->
        <div class="ll-obj on">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>My cat won't climb that step. How do cats get into it?</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">−</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            The entry opening sits at the top of the drum — your cat steps up and enters through the opening. Most cats will use any litter box they feel safe standing inside. During the first few days, many owners place the LitterLoops™ next to the existing litter box and let the cat explore at its own pace before making the full switch.
            <br><br>
            That said, cats with mobility issues, senior cats, or very young kittens may find a raised entry challenging. If your cat has limited mobility, we recommend checking the entry height against your cat's needs before purchasing.
            <span class="ll-obj-note">Honest limitation: Not ideal for senior or mobility-impaired cats</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>What about urine? Does wet litter work with the sieve?</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            Yes, with clumping litter. When your cat urinates, the moisture is absorbed and forms a solid clump over several minutes. When you rotate the drum after this, the sieve catches the solid clump and directs it into the collection tray.
            <br><br>
            Wait a few minutes after your cat uses the box before rotating — this allows the clump to fully solidify before the separation step.
            <span class="ll-obj-note">Important: Must use clumping litter</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>Does litter stick to the bottom? People online say this is a problem.</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            If litter is filled to the correct depth (approximately 2.5–3 inches, as shown in the included guide), clumps tend to form within the litter layer before reaching the floor. The smooth interior surface helps clumps roll freely when the drum rotates.
            <br><br>
            However, if a cat urinates directly against the basin wall at a shallow litter level, occasional residue may stick and will need a quick manual wipe during routine cleaning. This is normal maintenance for any litter box, including electric models.
            <span class="ll-obj-note">Honest limitation: Occasional manual cleaning still needed</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>Does the drum move when my cat steps in? Will it feel unstable?</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            No. The drum sits in a fixed base cradle and only rotates when you manually turn it. It does not spin under your cat's weight. When your cat is inside using the box, the unit stays completely stationary. The base includes non-slip feet to prevent the unit from sliding on smooth floors.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>What litter types can I use? (People are asking about wood pellets, tofu, crystals, pine.)</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            <strong>Clumping litter — YES:</strong> Fine-grain clumping clay, clumping corn, clumping cassava, and fine plant-based clumping litters all work because they form solid clumps that the sieve can separate.
            <br><br>
            <strong>Non-clumping litter — NO:</strong> Large wood pellets, paper pellets, non-clumping crystals, and any litter that does not form solid clumps will pass through the sieve along with clean litter. The separation mechanism depends entirely on solid clumps forming.
            <br><br>
            <em>If you currently use non-clumping litter, LitterLoops™ will require you to switch to a fine-grain clumping type.</em>
            <span class="ll-obj-note">Key requirement: Fine-grain clumping litter only</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>Does rotating it create a cloud of litter dust?</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            Some dust movement can occur during rotation — this is true of any litter disturbance, including traditional scooping. To minimize this, rotate the drum slowly and steadily rather than quickly. Using a low-dust clumping litter brand will also significantly reduce the amount of airborne particles.
            <br><br>
            If you or a member of your household is particularly sensitive to litter dust, wearing a mask during rotation is a reasonable precaution.
            <span class="ll-obj-note">Tip: Rotate slowly and use a low-dust clumping litter</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>Will it work for large cats? People are saying it looks too small.</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            Please check the confirmed dimensions in the product listing or packaging to assess fit for your specific cat. If you have a larger breed — Maine Coon, Norwegian Forest Cat, Ragdoll, or similarly sized cats — comparing the interior drum diameter against your cat's size before purchasing is strongly recommended.
            <br><br>
            Based on comments from the community, some owners of large cats feel the space is adequate and others feel it is too small. This depends entirely on your individual cat's size.
            <span class="ll-obj-note">Recommendation: Confirm dimensions for large breeds before purchasing</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>The waste tray looks small. How fast does it fill up?</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            For a single cat, the drawer typically needs emptying every 3 to 7 days depending on your cat's habits and litter usage. For households with 2 or more cats, plan to empty it every 2 to 3 days.
            <br><br>
            The drawer is intentionally sized to hold several days of waste for one cat. If you have multiple cats, you will need to empty it more frequently — this is an honest trade-off compared to larger electric models with bigger receptacles.
            <span class="ll-obj-note">Multiple cats: empty more frequently (every 2–3 days)</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>Does it control odor? It's an open design.</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            LitterLoops™ uses an open, ventilated design that cats feel comfortable entering. The waste drawer contains separated clumps away from the surface, which helps. However, because the design is open-top, it does not seal in odors the way some enclosed automatic boxes do.
            <br><br>
            The most effective odor management with LitterLoops™ is to rotate daily and empty the drawer regularly. Using an odor-control clumping litter brand will also help significantly.
            <span class="ll-obj-note">Honest: Does not seal odors — regular rotation is essential</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>This concept is 30 years old (Omega Paw). Why buy this?</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            You are right that the rotating sieve principle for litter boxes has existed for decades — and that is actually a strong argument <em>for</em> it. The core mechanism is proven. LitterLoops™ is a refined version of that same concept: a cleaner aesthetic, smooth rotation action, and a slide-out waste drawer for mess-free disposal.
            <br><br>
            If you want a non-electric rotating litter box and the concept appeals to you, the fact that this design has worked reliably for 30 years is a feature, not a flaw.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>Do I still need to manually scoop or clean anything?</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            Yes. LitterLoops™ significantly simplifies the daily waste removal process — but it does not replace all cleaning. You will still need to periodically rinse the basin and components, and occasionally spot-clean any residue that sticks to the interior.
            <br><br>
            It is a simpler routine, not a hands-free one. We believe being clear about this upfront reduces the chance of disappointment after purchase.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ll-obj">
          <div class="ll-obj-q">
            <span>Is it safe? No risk of hurting the cat like some electric boxes?</span>
            <div class="ll-obj-icon">+</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ll-obj-a">
            Because LitterLoops™ has no motor, no automatic cycling, and no electronic sensors, it cannot accidentally activate while your cat is inside. Rotation only happens when you manually turn it — and you can see at a glance whether your cat is still in the box.
            <br><br>
            This is one of the reasons some owners specifically prefer a non-electric design.
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
